Title 
Design and Implementation of a University Database System Using MySQL/PostgreSQL

Objectives 
● To understand the fundamentals of database design and relational database concepts. 
● To create and implement a relational database using MySQL/PostgreSQL. 
● To practice defining entities, attributes, and relationships between entities. 
● To implement one-to-many and many-to-many relationships. 
● To perform data insertion, retrieval, and queries for practical use cases. 

Problem Statement 
The aim of this project is to design a University Management System Database that can 
manage information about students, instructors, courses, and enrollments. The system must 
maintain the relationships between these entities: 
● Students can enroll in multiple courses. 
● Courses can have multiple students. 
● Instructors can teach one or more courses. 

Software and Hardware Requirements 

Software Requirements: 
● MySQL Server  
● MySQL Workbench  
● Operating System: Ubuntu 

Hardware Requirements: 
● Processor: Intel i3 or higher 
● RAM: 4 GB or higher 
● Hard Disk: 50 GB free space 
● Internet connectivity (for downloading software and updates) 


Theory / Concept (Brief) 
1. Relational Database 
A relational database stores data in tables (relations) with rows and columns. Each table 
represents an entity. 
2. Entities and Attributes 
o Student: Represents a student with attributes like StudentID, Name, Email, Age, 
Address. 
o Instructor: Represents a faculty member with InstructorID, Name, Email, 
Department. 
o Course: Represents a course with CourseID, CourseName, Credits, InstructorID. 
o Enrollment: Connects students and courses for many-to-many relationships. 
3. Relationships 
o One-to-Many: An instructor can teach multiple courses. 
o Many-to-Many: Students can enroll in multiple courses, and courses can have 
multiple students. Implemented via the Enrollment table. 
4. Primary and Foreign Keys 
o Primary Key: Uniquely identifies each row in a table. 
o Foreign Key: Ensures referential integrity between related tables. 
5. Normalization 
Ensures that data redundancy is minimized, and relationships are properly defined. 


Conclusion 
The University Database System allows efficient management of students, courses, and 
instructors. By implementing primary and foreign key constraints, the database ensures data 
integrity and supports complex queries. This project reinforces the concepts of relational 
databases, entity-relationship modeling, and normalization, providing practical experience in 
database design and implementation. 
